<RevealLayout
  title="The Rubik's Cube"
  description="Efficient representations and traversal of the Rubik's Cube state space"
  theme="dark"
>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        This is a Rubik's Cube. Six faces, nine stickers each, fifty-four
        colours in total. You have probably held one at some point. Maybe you
        solved it once. Maybe you gave up, peeled the stickers off, and called
        it a day.
      </p>
      <p>
        Today we are not going to talk about how to solve it using our fingers,
        but using a computer.
      </p>
    </aside>
    Hook
  </section>

  <section id="intro">
    <LogoAnimation
      client:load
      width={2080}
      height={600}
      pixelScale={8}
      duration={2700}
      loop={true}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="cube">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        The first step is to represent the cube in a way that a computer can
        understand. We need to encode the state of the cube — which stickers are
        where — into a data structure. There are many ways to do this, but a
        straightforward approach is to use a string of characters, where each
        character represents a sticker's colour and position. For example, we
        can list the stickers in a specific order. This way, we can easily
        manipulate the string to simulate moves and track the cube's state as we
        solve it.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RubikCubeViz
      client:only="react"
      width={1080}
      height={720}
      showStateEditor={true}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="lehmer-interactive">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        To encode a permutation as a single number we use Lehmer codes. Drag the
        coloured blocks to reorder them and watch how the Lehmer code and the
        factoriadic index update in real time.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <LehmerInteractive client:only="react" initialN={3} showNSelector={true} />
  </section>

  <section id="lehmer-table">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Here is the complete table of all permutations for a given n. Each row
        shows the colour arrangement, its Lehmer code, and the factoriadic index
        computed from it.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <LehmerTable
      client:only="react"
      initialN={3}
      showNSelector={true}
      maxHeight={520}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p></p>
    </aside>
    <div style={{ height: "80vh" }}>
      <PyodideWorkerRunner
        client:only="react"
        initialCode={stringRepresentationCode}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="state-graph">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Now let's visualise the state space as a graph. Each node is a cube
        state and each edge is a single move connecting two states. You can type
        any state index, adjust the BFS depth with the slider, and explore the
        neighbourhood. Zoom in to see the unfolded cube on each node; zoom out
        and they collapse to dots. Click a node to re-centre the graph on it.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RubikStateGraph
      client:only="react"
      initialIndex={0}
      initialDepth={1}
      overflow={true}
    />
  </section>
</RevealLayout>
